            <ArticleTitle>Gabapentin for the treatment of neuropathic pain in a pregnant horse.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>755-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE DESCRIPTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 24-year-old 732-kg (1,610-lb) pregnant Belgian draft horse mare developed neuropathy and signs of intractable pain following colic surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CLINICAL FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Following recovery from colic surgery to treat compression of the small and large intestines because of a large fetus, the mare was noticed to have signs of femoral neuropathy involving the left hind limb. Within 36 hours after recovery, the mare developed signs of severe pain that were unresponsive to conventional treatment. No gastrointestinal tract or muscular abnormalities were found, and the discomfort was attributed to neuropathic pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="TREATMENT AND OUTCOME" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The mare was treated with gabapentin (2.5 mg/kg [1.1 mg/lb], PO, q 12 h). Shortly after this treatment was initiated, the mare appeared comfortable and no longer had signs of pain. Treatment was continued for 6 days, during which the dosage was progressively decreased, and the mare was discharged. The mare subsequently delivered a healthy foal.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CLINICAL RELEVANCE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Gabapentin appeared to be a safe, effective, and economical treatment for neuropathic pain in this horse.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
